  "summary": "Redwood City Council voted on Oct. 5 to approve its response to a San Mateo County Civil Grand Jury report on the city's ALPR program, agreeing with all seven findings and outlining policy changes to audits, retention, and data sharing. In response to the report, the Redwood City Police Department revised its ALPR policy and terminated its data-sharing relationship with Stanford University's Department of Public Safety on Aug. 10, after prior disclosures that four entities had improperly searched city ALPR data at least 99 times. The current Flock Safety contract is set to expire Oct. 31, 2026.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "the city terminated the data-sharing relationship with Stanford University Department of Public Safety on Aug. 10",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    },
    {
      "quote": "four entities improperly searched city license plate reader data at least 99 times",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "the city's current agreement with Flock Safety will expire on Oct. 31, 2026",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "We can make our law enforcement officers' lives a lot easier by discontinuing the use of ALPRs and removing the need for audits altogether.",
      "paragraph_idx": 23
